### **Here are the most common types of buttons:**

If you aren't sure, here are the three most popular types. You can copy the one that fits your needs.

#### 1. HTML & CSS (For a Website)
This creates a standard, styled button that links to a website.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<style>
  /* Style the button */
  .my-button {
    background-color: #4CAF50; /* Green */
    border: none;
    color: white;
    padding: 15px 32px;
    text-align: center;
    text-decoration: none;
    display: inline-block;
    font-size: 16px;
    margin: 4px 2px;
    cursor: pointer;
    border-radius: 8px; /* Rounded corners */
    transition: background-color 0.3s;
  }

  /* Change color on hover */
  .my-button:hover {
    background-color: #45a049;
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<h2>Website Button Example</h2>

<!-- Button that links to Google -->
<a href="https://www.google.com" target="_blank">
  <button class="my-button">Go to Google</button>
</a>

<!-- Button that runs a JavaScript alert -->
<button class="my-button" onclick="alert('Hello! You clicked me.')">Click Me</button>

</body>
</html>
```

#### 2. JavaScript / React (For Web Apps)
If you are using React, here is a functional button component.

```jsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';

function MyButton() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  return (
    <button 
      onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)} 
      style={{
        padding: '10px 20px',
        fontSize: '16px',
        backgroundColor: '#007bff',
        color: 'white',
        border: 'none',
        borderRadius: '5px',
        cursor: 'pointer'
      }}
    >
      Clicked {count} times
    </button>
  );
}

export default MyButton;
```

